      "math_academy_id": "q-343389",
      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-5{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-15t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{540(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
